 To force a textfield in Adobe Animate (formerly Adobe Flash Professional) to display columns, you can use ActionScript 3.0. Here's a simple example of how to create a text field with two columns:

1. First, create a new text field in your Adobe Animate project.

2. Select the text field and open the Actions panel (Window > Actions).

3. In the Actions panel, paste the following code:

```
import flash.text.TextFormat;
import flash.text.TextFieldAutoSize;

var columnWidth:Number = 200; // Set the width of each column
var lineWidth:Number = columnWidth; // Set the width of each line within a column
var numColumns:int = 2; // Set the number of columns

var textFormat:TextFormat = new TextFormat();
textFormat.wordWrap = true;
textFormat.lineWidth = lineWidth;
textFormat.wordWrapWidth = columnWidth;

myTextField.defaultTextFormat = textFormat;
myTextField.autoSize = TextFieldAutoSize.LEFT;
myTextField.multiline = true;

myTextField.text = "Your text here...";
```

Replace `myTextField` with the name of your text field. Adjust the `columnWidth`, `lineWidth`, and `numColumns` variables to fit your specific needs.

This code sets the text format, word wrapping, and auto-sizing properties of the text field to create columns. The text will automatically wrap to the next line and column as needed.

target_response:
 In TypeScript, both interfaces and types are used to define the structure of data, but they serve slightly different purposes and have different use cases.

1. Type: A type is a basic building block used to define the data that a variable, function parameter, or property can hold. Types can be simple, like string, number, boolean, or any, or more complex, like a union type or a tuple.

   Example:
   ```
   let myVariable: string;
   function myFunction(param: number): void { }
   const myTuple: [number, string] = [1, 'two'];
   ```

2. Interface: An interface is a way to define a contract or structure for an object, class, or value. It describes the shape of an object, including its properties and methods. Interfaces can be used with classes to ensure that the class implements all the properties and methods defined in the interface.

   Example:
   ```
   interface Person {
     name: string;
     age: number;
     sayHello: () => void;
   }

   class User implements Person {
     name: string;
     age: number;

     constructor(name: string, age: number) {
       this.name = name;
       this.age = age;
     }

     sayHello() {
       console.log(`Hello, ${this.name}!`);
     }
   }
   ```

In summary, use a type when you want to define a variable, function parameter, or property's data type, and use an interface when you want to define a contract or structure for an object, class, or value.

