 To create a new line within a cell in a LaTeX table, you can use the `\\` command. However, this command is typically used to create a new row, not a new line within a cell. If you want to create a new line within a cell for multi-line text, you can use the `parbox` command. Here's an example:

```
\documentclass{article}
\usepackage{parbox}

\begin{document}

\begin{table}
\centering
\begin{tabular}{|c|}
\hline
\parbox{4cm}{
Multiline text \\
in a cell \\
of the table \\
} \\
\hline
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

\end{document}
```

In this example, the `\parbox{4cm}{...}` command creates a box of width 4cm, and the text within it will be wrapped into multiple lines if necessary. The `\\` command is used to create a new line within the box. The `\hline` command is used to create a horizontal line in the table.

