 In C++, you can use a loop to run the same code on multiple named variables. Here's a concise way to do it using a `std::map` or `std::unordered_map` to store the variables and their values, and a `for_each` function from the `<algorithm>` library to apply the same operation to each pair in the map.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <map>
#include <algorithm>

int main() {
    std::map<std::string, int> variables = {
        {"var1", 1},
        {"var2", 2},
        {"var3", 3}
    };

    // Define the operation to perform on each variable
    auto operation = [](int& value) { value *= 2; };

    // Apply the operation to each variable in the map
    std::for_each(variables.begin(), variables.end(), [&operation](auto& pair) {
        operation(pair.second);
    });

    // Print the updated values
    for (const auto& pair : variables) {
        std::cout << pair.first << ": " << pair.second << std::endl;
    }

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `operation` function multiplies each value by 2. You can replace it with any operation you need. The `for_each` function applies the operation to each value in the `variables` map. The updated values are then printed out.

